/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Unit step u(t) is 0 for t<0 and 1 for t≥0.
   - x(t) is the signal being sampled; it must be continuous at t=t0 for this to hold. t0 is the shifting instant; the integral is over all time.
   - Unit impulse δ(t) is not an ordinary function.
   - In discrete time δ[n]=1 at n=0 and 0 elsewhere.
   - r(t) is the continuous-time unit ramp; r[n] is the discrete-time unit ramp; u is the unit step.
   - Unit ramp r(t)=t u(t): r(t)=0 for t<0 and r(t)=t for t≥0.
   - e is the base of natural logarithms; j=√−1; ω is angular frequency in rad/s; t is time in s.
   - Real exponential x(t)=A eat u(t) with A and a real.
   - In discrete time the complex exponential is ejΩn, with Ω in rad/sample; it is periodic only when Ω/(2π) is rational.
   - Any piecewise signal made of straight segments and jumps can be written with steps and ramps.
*/
