/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write y(t) = x(t − t₀).
   - For t₀ > 0 every feature moves right, so the signal is delayed.
   - Discrete time uses the same rule with an integer shift: y[n] = x[n − n₀], with n₀ a whole number.
   - Shifting models every delay: an audio echo, a satellite hop, the queue buffer inside a router, a UPI confirmation that lands two seconds late.
   - Write y(t) = x(at), with a a nonzero real number.
   - a is the scale factor; a > 1 compresses time (faster, shorter signal), 0 < a < 1 stretches time (slower, longer signal), and a < 0 combines scaling with reversal.
   - A four-minute song played at double speed is x(2t); it finishes in two minutes.
   - Discrete time needs an integer index, so scaling splits in two. y[n] = x[Mn] with M a positive integer keeps every M-th sample — downsampling, or decimation — and the record becomes M times shorter.
   - Write y(t) = x(−t).
   - x(−t) flips the time axis, not the amplitude.
*/
