/* book exam traps: x(2t − 1) does not mean a delay of 1 s. Shifting x right by 1 s gives x(t − 1); replacing t by 2t then gives x(2t − 1), which is the same waveform, but the compression shrinks the 1 s delay to 0.5 s. Compressing first by 2 and then shifting right by 0.5 s give | In x(at − b) the delay is b/a seconds, not b. | x(−t) is not −x(t). The first mirrors the signal in time, the second flips it upside down. A pulse on 0 to 2 s becomes a pulse on −2 to 0 s, not a dip on 0 to 2 s. | For discrete time, the shift n₀ must be an integer and the scale M must be a positive integer. x[2n] keeps samples 0, 2, 4, … and is not interpolation; zero insertion is the separate upsampling step. | Sign slip on shifts: x(t + 3) moves the signal 3 s to the left, because the feature at t₁ now occurs at t₁ − 3. */
